msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr "Background {#background}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Full quantum state tomography of an $N$-qubit system requires exponentially many measurements to estimate all $4^N -1$ independent parameters of the density matrix. For a system of 50 qubits, for example, this process requires on the order of $10^{30}$ shots, which is more than a billion times the number of stars in the observable universe. Shadow tomography [\\[1\\]](#references) is a quantum information protocol that constructs a “classical shadow” of the quantum state, from which target observables can be estimated by using a number of shots that scales logarithmically in the number of observables and is independent of system size $N$, making it relevant across many areas in quantum computing, including energy estimation, entanglement entropy and correlation function calculations, kernel estimation in quantum machine learning, and others.\n"
msgstr "Ang buong quantum state tomography ng isang $N$-qubit na sistema ay nangangailangan ng exponential na dami ng measurement para i-estimate ang lahat ng $4^N -1$ na independent na parameter ng density matrix. Para sa sistemang may 50 qubit, halimbawa, nangangailangan ang prosesong ito ng humigit-kumulang $10^{30}$ shot, na higit sa isang bilyong beses ng bilang ng mga bituin sa observable universe. Ang shadow tomography [\\[1\\]](#references) ay isang quantum information protocol na bumubuo ng “classical shadow” ng quantum state, kung saan maaaring i-estimate ang mga target na observable gamit ang bilang ng shot na logarithmic ang scaling sa bilang ng mga observable at independent sa laki ng sistemang $N$, kaya mahalaga ito sa maraming larangan ng quantum computing, kabilang ang energy estimation, entanglement entropy at correlation function calculation, kernel estimation sa quantum machine learning, at iba pa.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial shows how to use shadow tomography to estimate local observables, such as the single-qubit magnetization $\\langle Z_i \\rangle$ for qubit $i$, the two-point correlation function $\\langle Z_i Z_j\\rangle$, and the two-qubit reduced density matrix (2-RDM), of the highly entangled Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) state. The tutorial first illustrates a small-scale shadow tomography workflow on a 10-qubit GHZ state by using statevector simulation. Then it shows a 100-qubit demonstration on real quantum hardware.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng tutorial na ito kung paano gamitin ang shadow tomography para i-estimate ang mga local observable, tulad ng single-qubit magnetization na $\\langle Z_i \\rangle$ para sa qubit na $i$, ang two-point correlation function na $\\langle Z_i Z_j\\rangle$, at ang two-qubit reduced density matrix (2-RDM), ng lubhang entangled na Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) state. Una, ipinapakita ng tutorial ang maliit na shadow tomography workflow sa 10-qubit na GHZ state gamit ang statevector simulation. Pagkatapos, nagpapakita ito ng 100-qubit na demonstrasyon sa totoong quantum hardware.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shadow tomography protocol {#shadow-tomography-protocol}"
msgstr "Shadow tomography protocol {#shadow-tomography-protocol}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The shadow tomography protocol has the following steps:\n"
"1. Apply a random unitary $U$, drawn from a unitary ensemble, to the initial quantum state.\n"
"2. Measure the outcome in the computational basis, obtaining a bitstring $\\ket{b}$.\n"
"3. Store the classical snapshot, $\\hat{\\rho} = \\mathcal{M}^{-1}(U^{\\dagger}\\ket{b}\\bra{b}U)$, where $\\mathcal{M}^{-1}$ is the inverse of the measurement channel $\\mathcal{M}(\\rho)=\\mathbb{E}[U^{\\dagger}\\ket{b}\\bra{b}U]$.\n"
"4. Repeat the process $T$ times, where the collection of $T$ snapshots $\\{\\hat{\\rho}_t\\}$ forms a classical shadow $S(\\rho, T)$.\n"
"5. Estimate observable $o$ of operator $O$ as $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\text{Tr}(O\\hat{\\rho}_t)$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang shadow tomography protocol ay may mga sumusunod na hakbang:\n"
"1. Mag-apply ng random na unitary na $U$, na kinuha mula sa isang unitary ensemble, sa initial quantum state.\n"
"2. Sukatin ang resulta sa computational basis, para makakuha ng bitstring na $\\ket{b}$.\n"
"3. Itabi ang classical snapshot, $\\hat{\\rho} = \\mathcal{M}^{-1}(U^{\\dagger}\\ket{b}\\bra{b}U)$, kung saan ang $\\mathcal{M}^{-1}$ ay ang inverse ng measurement channel na $\\mathcal{M}(\\rho)=\\mathbb{E}[U^{\\dagger}\\ket{b}\\bra{b}U]$.\n"
"4. Ulitin ang proseso nang $T$ beses, kung saan ang koleksyon ng $T$ snapshot na $\\{\\hat{\\rho}_t\\}$ ay bumubuo ng classical shadow na $S(\\rho, T)$.\n"
"5. I-estimate ang observable na $o$ ng operator na $O$ bilang $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\text{Tr}(O\\hat{\\rho}_t)$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shadow tomography variants {#shadow-tomography-variants}"
msgstr "Mga variant ng shadow tomography {#shadow-tomography-variants}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The choice of the unitary ensemble, that is, basis, in step 1 of the protocol, affects the sample complexity, variance of estimates, and information completeness of a given shadow tomography. Many ensemble groups have been considered in the literature, each with trade-offs between complexity and observable estimation efficiency. The properties of the most commonly used variants are summarized in the following table, although this list is not exhaustive.\n"
msgstr "Ang pagpili ng unitary ensemble, ibig sabihin ang basis, sa hakbang 1 ng protocol, ay nakakaapekto sa sample complexity, variance ng mga estimate, at pagkakumpleto ng impormasyon ng isang shadow tomography. Maraming ensemble group ang nasuri na sa literatura, na bawat isa ay may mga trade-off sa pagitan ng complexity at kahusayan ng observable estimation. Ibinubuod sa sumusunod na talahanayan ang mga katangian ng mga pinakakaraniwang ginagamit na variant, bagaman hindi kumpleto ang listahang ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Variant | Ensemble | Sample complexity | Circuit depth | Suitable observables |\n"
"| ------------ | ---------------------------------| ---------| ---| -------- |\n"
"| local Pauli [\\[1\\]](#references) | single-qubit Paulis $\\{X, Y, Z\\}$ | $\\mathcal{O}(3^k\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | 1 | local/few-body |\n"
"| global Clifford [\\[1\\]](#references) | $N$-qubit Clifford |$\\mathcal{O}(\\text{Tr}(O^2)\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | $\\mathcal{O}(N\\text{log}N)$ | low-rank, fidelity |\n"
"| fermionic Gaussian [\\[2\\]](#references), [\\[3\\]](#references)| matchgate circuits | $\\mathcal{O}(\\binom{\\mu}{k}\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon^2})$  | $\\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ | fermionic |\n"
"| shallow shadows [\\[4\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references) | low-depth circuits| $\\mathcal{O}(k2^k\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | $\\mathcal{O}(\\text{log}N)$ | non-local (hardware-friendly) |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Variant | Ensemble | Sample complexity | Circuit depth | Angkop na mga observable |\n"
"| ------------ | ---------------------------------| ---------| ---| -------- |\n"
"| local Pauli [\\[1\\]](#references) | single-qubit Paulis $\\{X, Y, Z\\}$ | $\\mathcal{O}(3^k\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | 1 | local/few-body |\n"
"| global Clifford [\\[1\\]](#references) | $N$-qubit Clifford |$\\mathcal{O}(\\text{Tr}(O^2)\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | $\\mathcal{O}(N\\text{log}N)$ | low-rank, fidelity |\n"
"| fermionic Gaussian [\\[2\\]](#references), [\\[3\\]](#references)| matchgate circuits | $\\mathcal{O}(\\binom{\\mu}{k}\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon^2})$  | $\\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ | fermionic |\n"
"| shallow shadows [\\[4\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references) | low-depth circuits| $\\mathcal{O}(k2^k\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | $\\mathcal{O}(\\text{log}N)$ | non-local (hardware-friendly) |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The terms used in the table are defined as follows:\n"
msgstr "Ang mga terminong ginamit sa talahanayan ay tinutukoy bilang sumusunod:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "\"sample complexity\" refers to the number of measurements needed for a given shadow tomography variant.\n"
msgstr "Ang \"sample complexity\" ay tumutukoy sa bilang ng mga measurement na kailangan para sa isang partikular na shadow tomography variant.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "\"circuit depth\" describes how expensive the measurements are to implement on quantum hardware.\n"
msgstr "Inilalarawan ng \"circuit depth\" kung gaano kamahal ipatupad ang mga measurement sa quantum hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$N$ is the number of qubits.\n"
msgstr "Ang $N$ ay ang bilang ng mga qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$k$ is the locality of the observable (for example, $\\langle Z_i \\rangle$ has $k=1$ and $\\langle Z_i Z_j\\rangle$ has $k=2$).\n"
msgstr "Ang $k$ ay ang locality ng observable (halimbawa, ang $\\langle Z_i \\rangle$ ay may $k=1$ at ang $\\langle Z_i Z_j\\rangle$ ay may $k=2$).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$M$ is the number of observables to be estimated simultaneously.\n"
msgstr "Ang $M$ ay ang bilang ng mga observable na sabay na ie-estimate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$\\varepsilon$ is the additive error tolerance.\n"
msgstr "Ang $\\varepsilon$ ay ang additive error tolerance.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$\\mu$ is the number of fermions.\n"
msgstr "Ang $\\mu$ ay ang bilang ng mga fermion.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Though the local Pauli ensemble is the most hardware-friendly, it pays a $3^k$ sample complexity penalty, which makes it ill-suited for non-local property estimation. More generally, the local-shadow sample-complexity bound scales as $4^k$, improving to $3^k$ in the special case of tensor-product observables.\n"
msgstr "Bagaman ang local Pauli ensemble ang pinaka-hardware-friendly, may kapalit itong $3^k$ na sample complexity penalty, kaya hindi ito angkop sa pag-estimate ng mga non-local na katangian. Mas pangkalahatan, ang local-shadow sample-complexity bound ay sumasabay sa $4^k$, na bumubuti sa $3^k$ sa espesyal na kaso ng mga tensor-product observable.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conversely, while the global Clifford ensemble can be used to estimate non-local observables, its circuit depth is not favorable. Fermionic Gaussian shadows offer utility in simulations of many-body physics, chemistry, and materials, but require matchgate circuits, which might carry significant overhead on general purpose gate-based devices.\n"
msgstr "Sa kabilang banda, bagaman magagamit ang global Clifford ensemble para i-estimate ang mga non-local na observable, hindi paborable ang circuit depth nito. Nag-aalok ang mga fermionic Gaussian shadow ng gamit sa mga simulation ng many-body physics, chemistry, at materials, pero nangangailangan ng mga matchgate circuit, na maaaring magdala ng malaking overhead sa mga general purpose na gate-based na device.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shallow shadows has a circuit depth between the local Pauli and global Clifford, making it a highly promising variant for practical implementation, though there are still some open questions regarding optimal circuit depth for a given observable.\n"
msgstr "Ang shallow shadows ay may circuit depth sa pagitan ng local Pauli at global Clifford, kaya isa itong lubhang promising na variant para sa praktikal na implementasyon, bagaman may ilang bukas pang tanong tungkol sa pinakamainam na circuit depth para sa isang partikular na observable.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As the frontier of shadow tomography research continues expanding, some of the most relevant problems to address include optimally choosing the measurement ensemble for a target set of observables, improving sample complexity bounds, and extending the protocol to time-evolved observables. Nonetheless, shadow tomography still presents one of the leading workflows for extracting maximal classical information from a quantum state using a minimum number of measurements, which makes it ideal for characterizing quantum states at scale where both full tomography and classical simulation are impossible.\n"
msgstr "Habang patuloy na lumalawak ang hangganan ng pananaliksik sa shadow tomography, kabilang sa pinakamahahalagang problemang tutugunan ang pinakamainam na pagpili ng measurement ensemble para sa target na hanay ng mga observable, pagpapabuti ng mga sample complexity bound, at pagpapalawak ng protocol sa mga time-evolved na observable. Gayunpaman, isa pa rin ang shadow tomography sa mga nangungunang workflow para kunin ang pinakamaraming classical na impormasyon mula sa isang quantum state gamit ang pinakakaunting measurement, kaya mainam ito sa pagkilala ng mga quantum state sa sukat kung saan imposible ang parehong buong tomography at classical simulation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The local Pauli ensemble {#the-local-pauli-ensemble}"
msgstr "Ang local Pauli ensemble {#the-local-pauli-ensemble}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial uses the random Pauli basis, that is, single-qubit Clifford gates, to collect shadows and estimate properties of the GHZ state. Note that the Pauli measurements are a great choice for estimating local observables (such as spin density, local correlation functions, and position probability density), but not global ones (such as total energy, momentum, and charge).\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit ng tutorial na ito ang random Pauli basis, ibig sabihin ang mga single-qubit Clifford gate, para mangolekta ng mga shadow at mag-estimate ng mga katangian ng GHZ state. Tandaan na mahusay na pagpipilian ang mga Pauli measurement para sa pag-estimate ng mga local observable (tulad ng spin density, local correlation function, at position probability density), pero hindi para sa mga global (tulad ng total energy, momentum, at charge).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here, a random Pauli basis $\\{X, Y, Z\\}$ per qubit is independently chosen and the corresponding single-qubit rotation is applied, followed by measurement:\n"
msgstr "Dito, independiyenteng pumipili ng random na Pauli basis na $\\{X, Y, Z\\}$ bawat qubit at ina-apply ang katumbas na single-qubit rotation, na sinusundan ng measurement:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n"
msgstr "$$\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{equation}\n"
"U = U_0 \\otimes U_1 \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes U_{n-1}, U_q \\in \\{I, H, S^{\\dagger}H\\}\n"
"\\end{equation}\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{equation}\n"
"U = U_0 \\otimes U_1 \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes U_{n-1}, U_q \\in \\{I, H, S^{\\dagger}H\\}\n"
"\\end{equation}\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For this ensemble, the measurement channel's inverse is:\n"
msgstr "Para sa ensemble na ito, ang inverse ng measurement channel ay:\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{equation}\n"
"\\hat{\\rho}_q=3U_q^{\\dagger}\\ket{b_q}\\bra{b_q}U_q - I\n"
"\\end{equation}\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{equation}\n"
"\\hat{\\rho}_q=3U_q^{\\dagger}\\ket{b_q}\\bra{b_q}U_q - I\n"
"\\end{equation}\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The factor of $3$ comes from averaging over the three Pauli bases, which produces a depolarizing channel with parameter $\\frac{1}{3}$. The full estimator then factorizes as a product of single-qubit estimators. As shown in the table, for $M$ observables, each of locality $k$, the number of Pauli shadows needed to estimate all observables to additive error $\\varepsilon$ with high probability is $\\mathcal{O}(\\frac{3^k\\text{log}(M)}{\\varepsilon^2})$. The system size does not affect the measurement cost as long as the observables remain local, so exponentially many observables can be estimated simultaneously with only logarithmic cost.\n"
msgstr "Ang factor na $3$ ay nagmumula sa pag-average sa tatlong Pauli basis, na gumagawa ng depolarizing channel na may parameter na $\\frac{1}{3}$. Pagkatapos, nagfa-factorize ang buong estimator bilang produkto ng mga single-qubit estimator. Gaya ng ipinapakita sa talahanayan, para sa $M$ na observable, na bawat isa ay may locality na $k$, ang bilang ng mga Pauli shadow na kailangan para i-estimate ang lahat ng observable hanggang additive error na $\\varepsilon$ nang may mataas na probabilidad ay $\\mathcal{O}(\\frac{3^k\\text{log}(M)}{\\varepsilon^2})$. Hindi nakakaapekto ang laki ng sistema sa gastos ng measurement basta nananatiling local ang mga observable, kaya maaaring sabay na i-estimate ang exponential na dami ng observable sa logarithmic na gastos lamang.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Median of means {#median-of-means}"
msgstr "Median of means {#median-of-means}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A plain mean over $T$ snapshots reduces the variance of a single-shot estimate of an observable as $\\frac{1}{T}$, but is sensitive to outliers. Median of means can be used to remedy this. The process involves the following:\n"
"- Splitting the $T$ snapshots into $K$ equally-sized groups\n"
"- Computing the mean within each group\n"
"- Returning the median across group means\n"
msgstr ""
"Binabawasan ng payak na mean sa $T$ snapshot ang variance ng single-shot na estimate ng isang observable bilang $\\frac{1}{T}$, pero sensitibo ito sa mga outlier. Magagamit ang median of means para lunasan ito. Kasama sa proseso ang sumusunod:\n"
"- Paghahati ng $T$ snapshot sa $K$ grupong pantay ang laki\n"
"- Pagkalkula ng mean sa loob ng bawat grupo\n"
"- Pagbabalik ng median sa mga mean ng mga grupo\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial performs local Pauli shadow tomography to estimate properties of the GHZ state, first using a 10-qubit simulated example and then demonstrating a 100-qubit hardware experiment.\n"
msgstr "Nagsasagawa ang tutorial na ito ng local Pauli shadow tomography para i-estimate ang mga katangian ng GHZ state, una gamit ang 10-qubit na simulated na halimbawa at pagkatapos ay nagpapakita ng 100-qubit na hardware experiment.\n"

