msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/shadow-tomography/extracted-outputs/31dc35ea-6554-4ca7-9c3b-0b5394c46e4e-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Assess shot scaling {#assess-shot-scaling}"
msgstr "Suriin ang shot scaling {#assess-shot-scaling}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this tutorial, 5,000 snapshots are used to estimate the observables. The shot scaling of shadow tomography is assessed by plotting the mean error of one of the estimated functions against different shot budgets. The error in the two-qubit correlator decays as $1/\\sqrt{T}$ as expected for the standard statistical convergence of a mean estimator. The importance of shadow tomography is that the constant prefactor in front of $1/\\sqrt{T}$ does not grow with the number of qubits for local observables, such as those examined in this tutorial.\n"
msgstr "Sa tutorial na ito, 5,000 snapshot ang ginagamit para tantiyahin ang mga observable. Sinusuri ang shot scaling ng shadow tomography sa pamamagitan ng pag-plot ng mean error ng isa sa mga tinantiyang function laban sa iba't ibang shot budget. Ang error sa two-qubit correlator ay bumababa bilang $1/\\sqrt{T}$, gaya ng inaasahan sa karaniwang statistical convergence ng isang mean estimator. Ang kahalagahan ng shadow tomography ay ang constant prefactor sa harap ng $1/\\sqrt{T}$ ay hindi lumalaki kasabay ng bilang ng qubit para sa mga local observable, tulad ng mga sinuri sa tutorial na ito.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
